from checkpoint import *
R1='bernsteingelfandgelfand1978local1'; R2='bernsteingelfandgelfand1978local2'
talk(428,'1977-10-03','S. I. Gelfand','P000719-P000720','Local algebra models for Harish–Chandra modules',r'The opening lecture introduces admissible \((\mathfrak g,K)\)-modules, generalized infinitesimal characters and the problem of replacing suitable blocks by modules over explicit algebras. The finite-generation condition is supplied in the next lecture. The example is a matrix order for \(\operatorname{PSL}_2(\mathbb R)\). The heading begins on P000719; P000718 belongs to the preceding Dickey--Novikov material. The final nonmathematical report is not an occurrence-index item.')
talk(429,'1977-10-10','S. I. Gelfand','P000721-P000724','Nice algebras and low-rank Harish–Chandra blocks',r'The lecture makes the category and equivalence problem precise, explains Morita equivalence, and gives analytic matrix orders for rank-one groups and \(\operatorname{SL}_3(\mathbb R)\). The later Bernstein--Gelfand--Gelfand notes identify the precise restrictions: bounded central support for the first algebraization theorem, and regular central characters and sufficiently small neighborhoods for the nice-algebra theorem. Quiver and matrix conventions in the notebook are not fully consistent.')
talk(430,'1977-10-24','S. I. Gelfand','P000725-P000729','Normalizer quotients and principal-series calculations',r'The lecture constructs the algebra acting on finitely many \(K\)-types as a normalizer quotient and illustrates its polynomial realization through the principal series of \(\operatorname{PSL}_2(\mathbb R)\). The notes contain a Casimir-sign error and inconsistent parameter conventions; these are kept separate from the later published theorem. The proposed computations for additional groups are a research program, without a specified central block.')
talk(431,'1977-10-24','I. M. Krichever','P000729-P000730','Commuting differential operators and their earlier history',r'Krichever relates the recently discussed spectral-curve method to the earlier papers of Burchnall, Chaundy and Baker. The short intervention distinguishes the existence of algebraic relations, the geometric classification of commuting rings, and explicit theta-function formulas. An unnamed historical reference cannot be securely identified.')
talk(432,'1977-10-31','S. P. Novikov','P000730-P000734','Instantons, their moduli and the twistor construction',r'Novikov surveys finite-action Yang--Mills fields, topological charge, self-duality, dimension and connectedness questions for instanton moduli, and the twistor interpretation. Several numerical and convention-dependent assertions need qualification. The connectedness question is a definite problem, subsequently solved, whereas classification of all Yang--Mills critical points is broader than classification of instantons.')
talk(433,'1977-11-15','Béla Sz.-Nagy','P000735-P000736','Common inner divisors and matrix reduction over Hardy functions',r'The talk develops a generic linear-combination lemma for inner divisors in \(H^\infty\), starting from inner--outer factorization and the failure of an unrestricted Bézout identity. It is closely matched by Sz.-Nagy\textquotesingle s 1976 paper, which credits the two-function lemma to Sherman. The polynomial-combination formulation and matrix-reduction application must be distinguished from a general Smith normal form over \(H^\infty\).')
talk(434,'1977-11-15','I. M. Krichever','P000736-P000737','Rational KP solutions and particle dynamics',r'The talk identifies rational decaying KP solutions with pole dynamics of a Calogero-type many-particle system and a commuting higher flow. It also records a Lax compatibility representation and a brief proposal about commutative subalgebras in a Weyl skew field. The published announcement was received in September 1977 and appeared in 1978; numerical coefficients in the notebook require checking against its normalization.')
talk(435,'1977-11-21','Yu. I. Manin','P000738-P000745','Linear algebraic construction of all instantons',r'The manuscript heading is 21/XI/1977, not the catalogue\textquotesingle s 21 September. Manin gives the twistor and finite-dimensional linear-algebra description of unitary instantons, including orthogonal projection, Hermitian Gram matrices and a local connection formula. The matching Drinfeld--Manin announcement was received 27 November 1977 and published in 1978. The question about arbitrary connections already had an affirmative universal-connection theorem.')
talk(436,'1977-11-28','L. D. Faddeev','P000746-P000752','Classical and quantum solitons',r'Faddeev compares particle and soliton descriptions, formulates asymptotic decomposition heuristics, and discusses sine-Gordon quantization, exact scattering, semiclassical determinants and mass corrections. The scope of the general solvability and resolution claims is not specified. Breathers are explicitly recognized in the notes, so they cannot be treated as a refutation of every formulation the speaker might have intended. P000753--P000754 begin an adjacent February 1978 Gelfand talk, treated with its continuation under record 438, rather than as Faddeev material.')
begin(428)
item(428,'P000719','definition','The category of Harish–Chandra modules',r'The notes describe a compatible action of \(G\) and \(\mathfrak g\), with finite multiplicities on restriction to \(K\), and denote the category by \(\mathcal C\).',r'For a real linear connected semisimple group \(G\), maximal compact subgroup \(K\), and complexified Lie algebra \(\mathfrak g\), a Harish--Chandra module is a finitely generated \(U(\mathfrak g)\)-module with compatible locally finite algebraic \(K\)-action and finite multiplicity of every irreducible \(K\)-type.',r'\citet[\S2]{'+R1+r'} state the full definition. The manuscript supplies finite generation on P000721; it is needed in the theorem that follows.',[R1],reconstruction=r'The notes here do not require a global action of \(G\) on the algebraic module; compatibility is between \(\mathfrak g\) and \(K\). Finite generation is imported from the next lecture, explicitly marked.',cls='E')
item(428,'P000719','problem','Algebraization of a bounded part of the Harish–Chandra category',r'Gelfand asks for a description of \(\mathcal C\) by representations of an associative algebra with finitely many generators and relations.',r'For a bounded connected open subset \(D\) of the spectrum \(\Theta\) of the center \(Z(U(\mathfrak g))\), seek an algebra \(Q\), finite as a module over the center, for which the category of Harish--Chandra modules with generalized infinitesimal characters in \(D\) is equivalent to the corresponding support-restricted finite-dimensional \(Q\)-module category.',r'The precise affirmative theorem is \citet[\S3, Theorem~1]{'+R1+r'}, presented to the Paris Academy on 9 January 1978 and printed in March 1978. It is local in central support, not an equivalence between the entire category and all modules over one finite-dimensional algebra.',[R1],reconstruction=r'The bounded central-support restriction is supplied by the theorem actually proved; the opening formulation does not state it.',cls='A',problem='GP-1977-20')
item(428,'P000719','remark','The Lorentz-group matrix-pair precedent',r'The notes recall the Gelfand--Ponomarev reduction for the Lorentz group to two operators \(A,B\) satisfying \(AB=BA=0\).',r'The cited precedent concerns explicit finite-dimensional matrix problems describing suitable Harish--Chandra blocks for the Lorentz group. The relations \(AB=BA=0\) alone, without the block, support, nilpotence and equivalence conventions, do not specify the whole representation category.',r'The later account \citet[\S6, case \(\operatorname{SL}_2(\mathbb C)\)]{'+R2+r'} confirms an explicit local matrix-order model. This verifies the direction of the precedent but not an unrestricted equivalence with the bare two-operator category.',[R2],cls='E',match='partial',confidence='medium')
item(428,'P000719-P000720','definition','Central characters and support',r'The center \(Z\) has spectrum \(\Theta\); the notes attach a subset \(\operatorname{supp}V\subset\Theta\) to a module.',r'For a Harish--Chandra module \(V\), its central support is the finite set of characters \(\theta:Z\to\mathbb C\) for which the generalized \(\theta\)-eigenspace is nonzero. Finite generation and admissibility imply that \(V\) is annihilated by a finite-codimensional ideal of \(Z\).',r'\citet[\S2]{'+R1+r'} formulate this central-finiteness statement and use it to define the categories attached to subsets of \(\Theta\).',[R1],cls='E')
item(428,'P000720','theorem','Decomposition by generalized infinitesimal character',r'The category attached to a set of characters is written as a sum of the single-character categories; distinct characters do not interact.',r'Every Harish--Chandra module is a finite direct sum of its generalized central-character submodules. A homomorphism between modules with disjoint generalized central supports is zero. The category is therefore the finite-support direct sum of its generalized-character blocks.',r'This is the central decomposition used in \citet[\S2]{'+R1+r'}. It follows from the primary decomposition of a module over a finite-dimensional quotient of the commutative center.',[R1],cls='E',literal='correct')
item(428,'P000720','theorem','An algebra finite over the center',r'For bounded \(D\), the notes state an equivalence \(\mathcal C_D\simeq\operatorname{mod}Q\), where \(Q\) is finite over \(Z\).',r'For bounded connected open \(D\subset\Theta\), there exists a \(Z\)-algebra \(Q\), finite over \(Z\), and a \(Z\)-linear equivalence \(\mathcal C_D\simeq\operatorname{Mod}_D Q\), where the right side consists of finite-dimensional modules whose central support lies in \(D\).',r'Exactly \citet[\S3, Theorem~1]{'+R1+r'}. The later analytic localization \(Q\otimes_Z\mathcal O(D)\) permits a category of all finite-dimensional modules of that localized algebra; this is a different formulation.',[R1],cls='A',problem='GP-1977-20')
item(428,'P000720','construction','A rank-three analytic matrix order for PSL2(R)',r'The example uses \(3\times3\) matrix-valued analytic functions with specified entries vanishing at a distinguished point.',r'Near the regular infinitesimal character of the trivial representation of \(\operatorname{PSL}_2(\mathbb R)\), the block category is represented by a matrix order \(R\subset M_3(\mathcal O(D))\). In the published left-module convention, with rows and columns labeled \(-1,0,1\), the entries \(a_{-1,0},a_{1,0},a_{-1,1},a_{1,-1}\) vanish at the centered parameter \(z=0\).',r'\citet[\S5]{'+R2+r'} give the exact three-type order in March 1978. The notebook\textquotesingle s row/column placement must be compared up to permutation and opposite-algebra convention, not silently identified entry by entry.',[R2],reconstruction=r'The published parameter and left-module convention are stated explicitly; the notebook changes orientation in later diagrams.',cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
save()
begin(429)
item(429,'P000721','definition','Finite generation and admissibility',r'The definition now requires finite generation over \(U(\mathfrak g)\), finite-dimensional \(K\)-isotypic multiplicity spaces and compatible actions; Harish-Chandra\textquotesingle s theorem is invoked.',r'A Harish--Chandra module has the three conditions just stated. For irreducible admissible representations in the usual reductive representation-theoretic setting, the \(K\)-finite vectors form such a module. This is not a theorem about arbitrary representations without a topology or admissibility hypothesis.',r'\citet[\S2]{'+R1+r'} use this precise algebraic category. The classical admissibility theorem is background to the construction, rather than the novel local-algebra statement.',[R1],cls='E')
item(429,'P000721','definition','The support criterion is a character, not its value',r'The support condition is written with \(\theta(z)\), followed by a common central eigenvector condition.',r'A character \(\theta\in\Theta\) lies in the support of \(V\) if its generalized eigenspace is nonzero. Since the central action factors through a finite-dimensional commutative algebra, this is equivalent to the existence of a nonzero vector on which every \(z\in Z\) acts as \(\theta(z)\). The scalar \(\theta(z)\) itself is not a point of \(\Theta\).',r'The correctly typed support definition appears in \citet[\S2]{'+R1+r'}.',[R1],literal='false as written')
item(429,'P000721','theorem','Separation of disjoint central supports',r'The notes repeat that modules with separated support have no morphisms between them and split into single-support pieces.',r'If \(\operatorname{supp}V\cap\operatorname{supp}W=\varnothing\), then \(\operatorname{Hom}_{(\mathfrak g,K)}(V,W)=0\); the primary decomposition of the central action is canonical and finite for each object.',r'The same construction is used in \citet[\S2]{'+R1+r'} and recurs here as preparation for a local equivalence.',[R1],literal='correct')
item(429,'P000721-P000722','definition','Equivalence of categories',r'Functors in opposite directions are described as inverse, with their compositions identified with the identity.',r'An equivalence consists of functors \(F:\mathcal C\to\mathcal D\), \(G:\mathcal D\to\mathcal C\), and natural isomorphisms \(GF\cong\operatorname{Id}_{\mathcal C}\), \(FG\cong\operatorname{Id}_{\mathcal D}\). Equality on the nose is unnecessary.',r'This is the standard categorical meaning of the equivalences in \citet[\S3]{'+R1+r'}; the notebook uses equality as shorthand.',[R1],match='partial')
item(429,'P000722','construction','Morita equivalence for a full matrix algebra',r'Finite-dimensional vector spaces are identified with modules over \(M_2(\mathbb C)\), by tensoring with the defining representation and recovering a multiplicity space.',r'The functors \(E\mapsto\mathbb C^2\otimes E\) and \(V\mapsto\operatorname{Hom}_{M_2(\mathbb C)}(\mathbb C^2,V)\) are mutually quasi-inverse equivalences between finite-dimensional vector spaces and finite-dimensional left \(M_2(\mathbb C)\)-modules.',r'The notebook gives an elementary Morita example; the use of such equivalences is part of the local-algebra setting in \citet[\S3]{'+R1+r'}. The scan of P000722 is truncated, so the precise handwriting of its formulas could not be independently recovered.',[R1],literal='correct',match='thematic only',verification='indirect')
item(429,'P000722','definition','Nice analytic algebras',r'A \ru{приятная} algebra lies inside matrix-valued analytic functions and has the full matrix algebra as generic fiber.',r'Let \(E\) be a connected complex manifold and \(B\) a finite-dimensional semisimple algebra. A nice algebra is an \(\mathcal O(E)\)-subalgebra \(R\subset B\otimes\mathcal O(E)\), containing scalar functions and becoming \(B\otimes\mathcal M(E)\) after extension to the field of meromorphic functions; the published definition includes the required finiteness over \(\mathcal O(E)\).',r'\citet[\S2]{'+R2+r'} give the definition and distinguish the stronger freeness condition called strongly nice.',[R2],cls='A')
item(429,'P000722','theorem','The local nice-algebra theorem',r'The category is described by a nice algebra on a domain with dimension equal to the real split rank.',r'If \(\theta\) is a regular infinitesimal character, a sufficiently small neighborhood \(D\) of \(\theta\) has \(\mathcal C_D\) equivalent to the finite-dimensional modules of a nice analytic algebra. The parameter manifold has dimension equal to the real split rank; in the split case the formulation uses \(E=D\).',r'\citet[\S2, Theorem~1]{'+R2+r'} prove this with the regular-character and local-neighborhood hypotheses. The theorem is not stated there for arbitrary singular characters or arbitrary bounded domains.',[R2],reconstruction=r'Regularity and the sufficiently small neighborhood are essential hypotheses supplied from the published theorem, not visible in the notebook formulation.',cls='A',problem='GP-1977-20')
item(429,'P000722','claim','The sl2 Casimir has a sign inconsistency',r'The quadratic central element is written with a negative \(H^2\) term.',r'For \([H,E_+]=2E_+\), \([H,E_-]=-2E_-\), \([E_+,E_-]=H\), the element \(C=4E_-E_++H^2+2H\) is central. Replacing \(+H^2\) by \(-H^2\) does not yield a central element in these conventions.',r'The local parameter in \citet[\S5]{'+R2+r'} is a shifted normalized central character. The explicit sign diagnosis follows directly from the Lie brackets and is an editorial calculation, not a claim that the published paper printed the notebook formula.',[R2],reconstruction=r'The correction uses the matrix generators and differential realization explicitly recorded on P000726 and P000728. P000722 itself has missing scan pixels.',cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated')
item(429,'P000723','construction','Generators of the rank-one matrix order',r'Nine generators are given as matrix units, with selected off-diagonal units multiplied by the local coordinate \(z\).',r'If \(R\subset M_3(\mathcal O(D))\) is the indicated order, its \(\mathcal O(D)\)-module generators are diagonal matrix units, ordinary matrix units in unrestricted positions, and \(z e_{ij}\) in positions required to vanish at \(z=0\). Multiplication is inherited from \(e_{ij}e_{kl}=\delta_{jk}e_{il}\).',r'This is the explicit order of \citet[\S5]{'+R2+r'}, up to the notebook\textquotesingle s changing row/column conventions. It determines all relations without relying on the inconsistent arrow labels.',[R2],cls='A')
item(429,'P000723-P000724','construction','The three-vertex quiver and its relations',r'Three idempotent spaces \(L_1,L_2,L_3\), arrows \(e_{12},e_{21},e_{13},e_{31}\), product relations and a nilpotent central coordinate are drawn.',r'For a left module over the matrix order, \(e_{ij}\) maps the \(j\)-th idempotent space to the \(i\)-th. The arrows between the central vertex and the other two obey the matrix-unit multiplication relations, with the relevant two-step products equal to the central parameter and mixed products determined by the remaining generators. For the single-character block, that parameter acts nilpotently.',r'\citet[\S5]{'+R2+r'} verify the order and hence its module presentation. The right-hand notebook diagram reverses the \(e_{13}\) and \(e_{31}\) directions relative to this convention, while retaining the other pair; it cannot be repaired by one global transpose.',[R2],cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated')
item(429,'P000724','construction','The rank-two order for SL3(R)',r'The algebra \(M_4\oplus M_3\oplus M_3\oplus\mathbb C\) is restricted on the three walls \(z_1=0\), \(z_2=0\), \(z_1+z_2=0\) by vanishing and identification of matrix entries.',r'Near the regular character of the trivial representation of \(\operatorname{SL}_3(\mathbb R)\), the local category is represented by a nice order in \((M_4\oplus M_3\oplus M_3\oplus\mathbb C)\otimes\mathcal O(D)\). On \(z_1=0\), \(a_{13}=a_{14}=a_{23}=a_{24}=b_{12}=b_{13}=0\) and the lower right \(2\times2\) blocks of \(a,b\) agree; on \(z_2=0\), \(a_{12}=a_{14}=a_{32}=a_{34}=c_{12}=c_{13}=0\), with \(a_{22}=c_{22},a_{24}=c_{23},a_{42}=c_{32},a_{44}=c_{33}\); on \(z_1+z_2=0\), \(b_{13}=b_{23}=c_{13}=c_{23}=0\), \(b_{33}=c_{33}\).',r'\citet[\S6, case \(\operatorname{SL}_3(\mathbb R)\)]{'+R2+r'} publish these explicit wall conditions. This is an exact match for the formulas, apart from the independent notebook diagram convention.',[R2],cls='A')
save()
begin(430)
item(430,'P000725-P000726','definition','Compact weights in the PSL2(R) example',r'For \(K\simeq\operatorname{SO}(2)\), the operator \(X_0=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)\) is said to have eigenvalues in \(2\mathbb Z\); admissibility, finite generation and bounded multiplicity are discussed.',r'For the displayed real infinitesimal generator, a complex algebraic \(K\)-module has eigenvalues in \(2i\mathbb Z\) when the central element \(-I\) acts trivially. Integer weights are eigenvalues of \(X_0/i\). Harish--Chandra modules have finite multiplicities and, in this rank-one setting, bounded multiplicities; the conventions must be fixed before writing the truncating polynomial.',r'\citet[\S5]{'+R2+r'} use integral labels for the \(K\)-types without confusing the normalized weight operator with the real compact generator.',[R2],cls='E',literal='false as written',match='partial')
item(430,'P000726','claim','The Casimir and bounded central support',r'The notes again write \(4E_-E_+-H^2+2H\) as central and use its eigenvalues to localize the category.',r'For the displayed standard \(\mathfrak{sl}_2\) generators the central element is \(C=4E_-E_++H^2+2H\). Fixing a bounded region of its generalized spectrum is a rank-one instance of restricting central support.',r'The central-support theorem is \citet[\S3, Theorem~1]{'+R1+r'}. The sign error is visible on the complete scan of P000726 and is a faithful notebook error.',[R1],cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated')
item(430,'P000726-P000727','construction','Morita functors in arbitrary matrix size',r'The equivalence with \(M_\ell(\mathbb C)\)-modules is written using tensor product and \(\operatorname{Hom}\).',r'For finite-dimensional left \(M_\ell(\mathbb C)\)-modules, evaluation gives a natural isomorphism \(\mathbb C^\ell\otimes\operatorname{Hom}_{M_\ell}(\mathbb C^\ell,V)\cong V\); these functors are inverse equivalences.',r'This is elementary Morita equivalence, used as the model for the functor in \citet[\S4]{'+R1+r'}.',[R1],literal='correct',match='thematic only')
item(430,'P000727','construction','A left ideal selecting finitely many compact weights',r'A left ideal \(J\) is generated by \(\prod_{n=-N}^{N}(X_0-2n)\).',r'With the normalized generator \(T=X_0/i\), let \(p_N(T)=\prod_{n=-N}^{N}(T-2n)\) and \(J=U(\mathfrak g)p_N(T)\). Then \(\operatorname{Hom}_{U(\mathfrak g)}(U(\mathfrak g)/J,V)\) identifies with \(\ker p_N(T)\), the sum of the selected \(K\)-weight spaces.',r'This is the rank-one version of the finite-\(K\)-type construction in \citet[\S4]{'+R1+r'}. The factor \(i\) is required if one retains the notebook\textquotesingle s real generator.',[R1],cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial')
item(430,'P000727','construction','The normalizer quotient algebra',r'The normalizer is called the \ru{наименьшая} subalgebra containing \(J\) in which \(J\) is two-sided, and \(Q=N/J\).',r'For a left ideal \(J\subset U\), the right normalizer \(N_U(J)=\{u\in U:Ju\subset J\}\) is the largest subalgebra of \(U\) containing \(J\) in which \(J\) is a two-sided ideal. The quotient \(N_U(J)/J\) is an algebra acting on vectors annihilated by \(J\), with the chosen left/right convention.',r'\citet[\S4]{'+R1+r'} use this normalizer construction. The scan explicitly says smallest; this is a faithful mathematical error, not a translation issue.',[R1],cls='A',literal='false as written',development='annotated')
item(430,'P000727','construction','The finite-K-type functor',r'The functor \(\tau\) retains the weight spaces with indices \(-N,\ldots,N\); \(Q\) acts on their sum.',r'For an admissible \((\mathfrak g,K)\)-module \(V\), the selected sum \(\tau_N(V)=\bigoplus_{|n|\leq N}V_{2n}\) is finite-dimensional. The normalizer quotient acts because \(Jv=0\) implies \(Juv=0\) for \(u\in N_U(J)\).',r'\citet[\S4]{'+R1+r'} define the corresponding functor for a finite set of \(K\)-types.',[R1],cls='A',literal='correct')
item(430,'P000727','theorem','A sufficiently large finite set of K-types detects the block',r'For bounded \(D\), taking sufficiently many compact weights is asserted to give the desired equivalence.',r'For a bounded central-support region \(D\), a sufficiently large finite set of \(K\)-types can be chosen such that the associated normalizer-quotient functor is an equivalence on \(\mathcal C_D\); the algebra is finite over the center.',r'\citet[\S4, Proposition~1]{'+R1+r'} prove this and thereby establish their main theorem. A finite set independent of an arbitrary unbounded support region is not asserted.',[R1],cls='A',problem='GP-1977-20')
item(430,'P000728','construction','Homogeneous-function principal series',r'Functions on \(\mathbb R^2\setminus\{0\}\) homogeneous of degree \(s\) realize \(T^s\); on the circle, even trigonometric polynomials give the \(K\)-finite part.',r'For \(f(tv)=|t|^s f(v)\), the even principal-series representation restricts to even Fourier modes on the circle. Differentiation gives \(E_+=x\partial_y\), \(E_-=y\partial_x\), \(H=x\partial_x-y\partial_y\), up to the overall action convention explicitly chosen.',r'The principal-series family underlies \citet[\S5, Proposition~2]{'+R1+r'} and the rank-one calculation of \citet[\S5]{'+R2+r'}.',[R1,R2],cls='E',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(430,'P000728','method','Polynomial dependence on the principal-series parameter',r'The matrix elements of the Lie algebra action in selected compact types are polynomial in \(s\), producing an algebra of matrix polynomials.',r'After a fixed compact-picture realization and choice of finitely many \(K\)-types, the action of the normalizer quotient in principal-series families depends polynomially on the inducing parameter. The resulting representation embeds the quotient into a finite-dimensional endomorphism algebra tensored with the coordinate ring of the parameter space.',r'\citet[\S5, lemma and Theorem~2]{'+R1+r'} prove precisely this polynomial realization.',[R1],cls='A')
item(430,'P000728','claim','Subquotients of principal series',r'The notes suggest that every Harish--Chandra module appears as a subquotient of a principal-series representation.',r'Every irreducible Harish--Chandra module is a subquotient of a suitable principal-series representation. This assertion does not say that every finite-length module, including nonsplit extensions with nonsemisimple central action, is a subquotient of one ordinary principal series with a fixed scalar infinitesimal character.',r'\citet[\S5, Proposition~2(c)]{'+R1+r'} state the irreducible result. The stronger literal formulation in the notes is unsupported and fails for modules with nonsemisimple central action.',[R1],cls='E',literal='false as written',development='annotated')
item(430,'P000728','claim','Casimir eigenvalues in the homogeneous realization',r'The eigenvalue is written \(-s^2+2s\), and reducibility is discussed at \(s=0,2\).',r'For the displayed differential generators and homogeneity \((x\partial_x+y\partial_y)f=sf\), the correctly signed Casimir acts as \(s(s+2)\). Replacing the parameter by its negative gives \(s(s-2)\). The notebook\textquotesingle s scalar and reducibility parameter cannot both be used with its stated homogeneity convention.',r'The published treatment \citet[\S5]{'+R2+r'} instead uses a normalized parameter with central coordinate \(z=s^2-1\). The present identity is a direct editorial computation, not an attribution of the notebook formula to that paper.',[R2],cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated')
item(430,'P000728-P000729','construction','The reducible principal series at the trivial character',r'At \(s=0\), the constant function gives the trivial constituent and two remaining pieces are indicated; a second, dual reducibility point is shown at \(s=2\).',r'At homogeneity degree zero, the even principal series contains the constants and has the two limiting lowest/highest-weight constituents as quotient. The contragredient extension occurs at degree \(-2\), not \(+2\), for this same homogeneity convention. The sequence is generally nonsplit.',r'\citet[\S5]{'+R2+r'} describe the three constituents and compute the local order from their selected \(K\)-types. Their normalized parameter differs from the notebook\textquotesingle s degree.',[R2],cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial')
item(430,'P000729','theorem','Generic Weyl reflection of the principal-series parameter',r'The notes relate \(T^s\) and \(T^{2-s}\) away from exceptional parameters.',r'For the homogeneous degree convention above, the generic intertwining equivalence is between degrees \(s\) and \(-s-2\). With the opposite parameter convention it is \(s\leftrightarrow2-s\); reducibility points require separate treatment, and a generic isomorphism need not extend as an invertible holomorphic family there.',r'\citet[\S5, Proposition~2(b)]{'+R1+r'} give the generic Weyl-orbit equivalence; \citet[\S5]{'+R2+r'} exploit it in the explicit order.',[R1,R2],cls='E',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(430,'P000729','construction','Three compact types near the trivial character',r'The three weights \(-1,0,1\) are enough for the explicit algebra near the trivial character.',r'With the normalized integral labels for the even \(K\)-types, retaining labels \(-1,0,1\) gives the local rank-three order for a sufficiently small neighborhood of the trivial infinitesimal character.',r'\citet[\S5]{'+R2+r'} explicitly choose these three types and compute the order.',[R2],cls='A',literal='correct')
item(430,'P000729','remark','Further groups proposed for explicit computation',r'After listing rank-one groups and \(\operatorname{SL}_3(\mathbb R)\), the notes suggest \(\operatorname{SL}_3(\mathbb C)\), \(\operatorname{SU}(2,1)\), \(\operatorname{SO}(4,1)\), \(\operatorname{Sp}_4(\mathbb C)\), and the harder \(\operatorname{SL}_4\).',r'This is a program to compute explicit local Harish--Chandra orders for additional groups. The central character, real form in the last example, neighborhood and equivalence convention are not all specified, so it is not one definite classification problem.',r'\citet[\S6]{'+R2+r'} publish the listed low-rank examples, but do not identify or solve a single additional problem with all the notebook\textquotesingle s proposed groups and unspecified blocks.',[R2],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='medium',status='As of 10 November 2026, the program is identifiable; the notes do not determine an exact additional problem whose full fate can be asserted.')
prob('GP-1977-20','Local algebraization of Harish–Chandra categories',r'For a real linear connected semisimple group and bounded central-support domain \(D\), construct an algebra finite over the center realizing \(\mathcal C_D\); near a regular infinitesimal character, realize it by an explicit nice analytic matrix order.',['1977-428.2','1977-428.6','1977-429.7','1977-430.7'],r'Solved in the stated scope by Bernstein, I. M. Gelfand and S. I. Gelfand, presented 9 January 1978 and published March 1978; the nice-algebra theorem requires a regular infinitesimal character and a sufficiently small neighborhood. As of 10 November 2026, no extension to arbitrary singular blocks is asserted here.',[R1,R2])
